~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~Flow

Step 1: User Opens Page
User visits:
http://localhost:3000/products

Next.js automatically maps:
app/products/page.tsx
to
/products


Step 2: app/products/page.tsx

Page Uses Hook and component

export default function ProductsPage() {
  const { products } = useProducts();

  return (
    <ProductList products={products} />
  );
}


ProductsPage
      │
      ▼
useProducts()


Step 3: Hook Contains Logic
hooks/useProducts.ts

export function useProducts() {
  const [products, setProducts] = useState([]);

  useEffect(() => {
    getProducts().then(setProducts);
  }, []);

  return { products };
}

Hook responsibility:
Fetch Data
Manage State
Loading State
Error State


Step 4: Hook Calls Service
getProducts()
services/product.service.ts

export async function getProducts() {
  const response =
    await api.get('/products');

  return response.data;
}

Responsibility:
Build API Requests
Handle Responses
NOT UI.
NOT state.

Step 5: Service Uses API Client
lib/api.ts

import axios from "axios";

export const api = axios.create({
  baseURL: process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL
});

Responsibility:
Shared Axios Configuration
Auth Headers
Base URL
Interceptors

Step 6: Backend
GET /products

goes to NestJS.

Controller
    │
    ▼
Service
    │
    ▼
Prisma
    │
    ▼
MySQL

returns:
[
  {
    "id": 1,
    "name": "iPhone"
  }
]


Step 7: Render Components
Back in:app/products/page.tsx

<ProductList products={products} />

components/ProductList

export function ProductList({
  products
}) {
  return (
    <>
      {products.map(product => (
        <ProductCard
          key={product.id}
          product={product}
        />
      ))}
    </>
  );
}


Then:
<ProductCard />
it renders:
Product Name
Price
Image

Flow:
ProductsPage(app/products/page.tsx)
      │
      ▼
ProductList(components/ProductList)
      │
      ▼
ProductCard(components/ProductCard)



